{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Fabric Medallion Platform — Senior Architecture Review",
  "description": "A focused walkthrough of evidence, layer contracts, decisions, reliability, security, capacity, and executable release controls.",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "How to use this review",
      "description": "In about ten minutes, follow the platform from evidence and layer contracts through architecture decisions, recovery, security, capacity, executable controls, and a sequenced roadmap. Keep observed state separate from proposed targets throughout."
    },
    {
      "title": "Start with evidence, not a technology diagram",
      "description": "The README separates observed metadata, inferred architecture, and proposed controls. This prevents a target pattern or synthetic sample from being presented as an achieved production outcome.",
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^## Evidence taxonomy$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Understand the current data plane",
      "description": "The observed flow is BRZ to SIL to GLD to a shared Direct Lake semantic model and two focused reports. Aggregate counts establish scope without exposing tenant IDs, endpoints, or proprietary definitions.",
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^## Current-state architecture$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn medallion names into contracts",
      "description": "Each layer declares ownership, grain, keys, load behavior, evolution, quality, recovery, and consumers. A layer exists because it owns a boundary—not because three medal names look complete on a diagram.",
      "file": "docs/e2e-architecture.md",
      "pattern": "^## Layer contract summary$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect the architecture decisions",
      "description": "ADRs preserve context, alternatives, consequences, and revisit triggers. This is the interview-ready layer: the design can be defended and changed when its assumptions stop holding.",
      "file": "docs/adr/001-medallion-layer-boundaries.md",
      "pattern": "^## Decision$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Separate run success from trusted publication",
      "description": "A completed pipeline is not automatically trusted data. The orchestration state machine keeps candidate validation and publication state separate, enabling a fail-closed gate and last-known-good recovery.",
      "file": "docs/orchestration.md",
      "pattern": "^## Run and publication state machines$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Classify failure before retrying",
      "description": "Transient, capacity, authentication, schema, DQ, and logic failures require different actions. Blindly retrying deterministic failures consumes capacity and extends time to diagnosis.",
      "file": "docs/orchestration.md",
      "pattern": "^## Failure classification and retry policy$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Make quality fail closed",
      "description": "Expected contract coverage is checked before rule outcomes. Zero results, missing results, duplicate results, null status, and unknown status all reject a candidate instead of becoming accidental passes.",
      "file": "docs/data-quality.md",
      "pattern": "^## Minimum blocking controls$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Connect SLOs to ownership and decisions",
      "description": "Reliability targets are explicitly proposed, not claimed. Each has a measurement window, response, recovery dependency, accountable role, and review cadence.",
      "file": "docs/reliability-operating-model.md",
      "pattern": "^## Reliability objectives$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Review security as several boundaries",
      "description": "Workspace, item, data-plane, semantic, and audience permissions are distinct. The target model separates workload identities from users and requires positive and negative access tests.",
      "file": "docs/security-governance.md",
      "pattern": "^## Identity and access matrix$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Size with evidence",
      "description": "Capacity guidance begins with workload classes and a measurement loop. Scale, scheduling, model split, materialization, and pre-aggregation are decisions with explicit cost and reliability trade-offs.",
      "file": "docs/capacity-cost.md",
      "pattern": "^## Capacity design loop$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Validate the canonical contract",
      "description": "The JSON contract separates observed inventory from target requirements. Its schema and semantic validator enforce layer order, DAG consistency, required release gates, proposed-target labels, and public-portfolio safety.",
      "file": "architecture/layer-contracts.json",
      "pattern": "\"targetContracts\":"
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect the fail-closed SQL pattern",
      "description": "The synthetic procedure proves expected contract coverage and legal publication state before approval. It persists rejection evidence and handles an approved replay as an explicit idempotent no-op.",
      "file": "samples/sql/data-quality-gate.sql",
      "pattern": "CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.usp_gate_gold_publish"
    },
    {
      "title": "Close on the remediation roadmap",
      "description": "The 30/60/90 plan sequences environment safety, measurable recovery and quality, then capacity and governance. Exit measures make the audit actionable without implying those outcomes are already achieved.",
      "file": "docs/current-state-assessment.md",
      "pattern": "^## 30/60/90-day remediation roadmap$"
    },
    {
      "title": "Translate the review into leadership action",
      "description": "You have moved from dated evidence to target contracts, failure containment, decision rights, cost/security trade-offs, and an executable release gate. A Senior/Lead discussion should now connect the highest-risk control gap to an owner, an exit measure, and a reversible first delivery increment."
    }
  ]
}
